The '''Kuvari people''' are an ethnic group of the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]], and one of the two principal ethnic groups of the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]]. They comprise an estimated 35.5 percent of the country's population — roughly 41,000 people as of 2025 — and are concentrated on [[Tumala]] and the smaller islands of the eastern chain, after the historical expulsion of the Kuvari from [[Bolodun]].

The Kuvari are the indigenous inhabitants of the islands that now form the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands. In the pre-colonial period they were trade contacts of [[Guajicutea]] rather than tributaries, preserving their own local leadership and maritime traditions. Under Burawi colonial rule the Kuvari were subordinated within the plantation economy, and after emancipation a violent conflict — the [[Kuvari War]] (1796–1801) — culminated in the killing of large numbers of Kuvari on Bolodun and the expulsion of the survivors to the outer islands. The Kuvari administration on Tumala, led for a time by [[Mariba Kuna]], persisted as a separate political community until the islands' federation into a single state.

== History ==

=== Pre-colonial period ===

The Kuvari were a maritime trading people of the eastern Capuyaquiran Sea. Their islands were trade contacts of Guajicutea, exchanging goods with the wider island network while maintaining their own local leadership and seafaring traditions. The Kuvari islands lay at the outer edge of the Capuyaquiran Sea, bringing them into contact with trade routes linking the region to the wider Adonian world.

=== Colonial rule and emancipation ===

Under the [[Badweyn Islands]] colony, managed by the [[Lacashe]] province of [[New Burawa]], the islands were developed as sugar plantations. The Kuvari population was subordinated within the colonial social hierarchy, which ranked the indigenous islanders below the Burawi colonial class and, after the beginning of the [[Burawi slave trade]] in 1533, below the enslaved Kaftian plantation workforce.

Following the [[Abolition of Slavery Act]] in 1789 and the subsequent [[Emancipation of Slaves Act (Capuyaquiran League)|Emancipation of Slaves Act]], freed Sudanese Kaftians were relocated to Bolodun, where the Kuvari population had lived under an oppressive social hierarchy. The resulting unrest escalated into the [[Kuvari War]] (1796–1801). With military support from the [[Capuyaquiran League]], the Sudanese Kaftian administration prevailed; the war culminated in the killing of large numbers of Kuvari on Bolodun and the expulsion of the survivors to [[Tumala]] and the outer islands.

=== Post-war division ===

After the war, the Kuvari community was administered separately from Bolodun, under a Kuvari leadership on Tumala. For most of the following two centuries the two communities remained estranged. Shared neglect by the rest of the League and the prejudice of other island communities encouraged a slow rapprochement. The Kuvari were one of the communities brought into the multiracial coalition that carried the islands to independence in 1926.

== Culture ==

The Kuvari speak [[Burawi]] as their primary language, but retain a heritage [[Kuvari language]] that has declined in everyday use. Kuvari identity is strongly tied to the eastern islands, in particular Tumala, and to the memory of the Kuvari War and the expulsion from Bolodun. The [[Kuvari Peoples Indigenous Party]] represents the community in the national politics of the islands.

